an chronic processes, moderately supporting myeloid neoplasm.", "Acute neurological symptoms (blurry vision, ataxia) in a patient with blasts suggest a phase transformation in chronic myeloproliferative disease (B3) rather than primary myeloid leukemia (B1), but this is not definitive.", "Neurological deficits are non-specific but more commonly reported with myeloid neoplasms (B1, B3) due to marrow failure and blasts vs lymphoid/plasma cell disorders (B2, B4)."], "evidence_against": ["High blast percentage in peripheral blood with constitutional symptoms and pancytopenia is a hallmark of acute myeloid leukemia or blast crisis, providing moderate support for myeloid neoplasm.", "Lack of classic CML features weakly reduces likelihood of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm but does not exclude blast crisis.", "Absence of CML-specific markers weakly reduces likelihood of blast crisis (B3) but does not exclude it as blast crisis can present without these features."], "unresolved_questions": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of chronic MPN (PV, ET, PMF) in past?", "Presence of splenomegaly or constitutional symptoms typical of chronic phase?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["BCR-ABL1 fusion gene testing", "JAK2, CALR, and MPL mutation analysis", "Peripheral blood smear for dysplasia"]}, "target_level": 2}
